    pattern: "When deductive/inferential reasoning and critical-thinking dispositions are the weakest line, the person reaches conclusions quickly and defends them, but cannot reliably check whether the conclusion actually follows from the premises or survives disconfirming evidence.",
    untreatedTrajectory: "The cost is not measured in test scores but in decisions. Butler, Pentoney & Bong (2017) found that critical-thinking ability predicted real-world negative life events (foreclosures, unplanned pregnancies, credit-card debt, DUIs, missed medication) MORE strongly than IQ did, and added variance beyond IQ. Butler (2012) found a correlation of about r = -.38 between critical-thinking scores and self-reported negative life events. So a person low on reasoning tends to accumulate avoidable bad outcomes across finance, health, and law, even when raw intelligence is average or high. Reasoning is a component of fluid intelligence, and weak fluid reasoning also depresses downstream learning in math and science (Green et al., 2017). Honest limitation: 'critical thinking' and 'logical/deductive reasoning' are overlapping-but-distinct constructs, and this mapping onto a single 'Logical' line is inferential; the studies measure thinking skills broadly, not one isolated faculty.",
    connectionCase: "Critical thinking is explicitly trainable, unlike g. Meta-analytic and intervention work (Halpern's framework; Abrami et al. reviews) shows structured instruction in argument analysis, hypothesis-testing, and considering the other side produces measurable gains, and Butler's own logic is that raising critical thinking should lower the rate of costly life decisions. Peers strong in this line supply the thing the weak-Logical person can't self-generate: someone who asks 'does that actually follow?' and 'what would prove you wrong?' Surrounding yourself with rigorous thinkers externalizes the missing check until it is internalized.",
    growthMeasures: "Critical thinking training yields moderate effect sizes in meta-analyses (Abrami et al. report averaged Hedges' g around 0.30-0.34 across hundreds of studies); Butler's cross-sectional link is r ~= -.38 between CT ability and negative life events.",
