    id: "starved-parenting", kind: "starvation",
    name: "The One Whose Children Pay The Tuition",
    highLines: [],
    lowLines: ["Parenting"],
    pattern: "The line specific to raising children — warmth, calibrated control, autonomy-granting, low hostility — is the weakest, expressed as harsh, coercive, psychologically controlling, or low-warmth parenting.",
    untreatedTrajectory: "Pinquart's large meta-analysis (integrating research across many studies of parenting dimensions and styles) finds harsh control and psychological control are associated with HIGHER child/adolescent externalizing problems, with these among the strongest associations, and warmth/behavioral control associated with lower problems; several links are bidirectional and predict CHANGE over time. Cross-cultural meta-analysis shows harsh parenting relates to more internalizing AND externalizing symptoms across every world region studied, with cross-lagged effects of harsh parenting on later child symptoms — evidence the direction runs at least partly parent-to-child. Longitudinal network analysis flags maternal hostility/harshness as the strongest predictors of rising conduct and internalizing problems. Untreated, the cost is externalized onto the child's mental health and conduct trajectory.",
    connectionCase: "Parenting is one of the most demonstrably trainable relational lines: evidence-based behavioral parenting programs reliably reduce harsh discipline and child conduct problems. Because several parenting-child links are bidirectional, raising the parent's warmth/control skill can shift the child's behavior and dampen the coercive cycle. Peer support and modeling from skilled parents (the substance of group parenting programs) supplies concrete alternatives to the harsh defaults.",
    growthMeasures: "Pinquart reports harsh/psychological control among the strongest correlates of externalizing; cross-cultural meta-analysis confirms harsh-parenting->symptom links with cross-lagged effects. Behavioral parent-training meta-analyses (e.g., Kaminski et al.) find reliable reductions in child disruptive behavior and improved parenting practices.",
